    <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">‹ <a href="/">The brief</a></nav>

    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="sec-mark">Colophon</div>
      <h1 class="sec-title">Built like the systems <em>it describes.</em></h1>
      <p class="sec-sub">
        A site that argues "claims up front, receipts attached" should hold
        itself to the same contract. This page is how it does.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose reveal">
      <h2>One source of truth</h2>
      <p>
        Every number, date, title, and link on this site renders from a single
        file — <code>src/_data/facts.yaml</code> — synced to résumé revision
        <strong>{{ facts.meta.resume_rev }}</strong>. Pages are <a href="https://www.11ty.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eleventy&nbsp;3</a>
        + Nunjucks templates that interpolate from it; if a claim isn't in the
        file, it doesn't ship.
      </p>

      <h2>The linter that fails the deploy</h2>
      <p>
        <code>scripts/receipts-lint.mjs</code> runs in CI on every push. It scans the
        built HTML for four kinds of problems: <strong>required claims that went
        missing</strong> (the headline receipts must appear on the homepage, verbatim),
        <strong>superseded claims that crept back in</strong> — figures from older résumé
        revisions are hard-forbidden by pattern — <strong>internal jargon</strong> that an
        external reader couldn't evaluate, and <strong>internal links that point at
        pages the build never emitted</strong>. A contradiction between this
        site and the résumé is a broken build, not an embarrassment someone
        eventually notices. The audit trail lives on
        <a href="/receipts/">the receipts ledger</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>No internal jargon</h2>
      <p>
        Everything here is described by what it does, never by its internal
        codename. A reader outside Microsoft can't evaluate an acronym, and a
        résumé that needs a glossary isn't evidence — it's a membership test.
        The translation table lives with the résumé source, and the linter
        enforces it: an internal name in the built HTML fails the deploy.
      </p>

      <h2>One résumé</h2>
      <p>
        There is exactly one résumé — no dossier of tailored cuts to choose
        from. The PDF is the canonical authored document, hosted at the same
        revision this site renders from, and the
        <a href="/resume/">/resume/</a> page is the same record with a print
        stylesheet, so what you print and what you download can't disagree.
      </p>

      <h2>Freshness is emitted, not maintained</h2>
      <p>
        The revision stamp and date in the footer — <strong>rev {{ build.rev }} ·
        {{ build.updated }}</strong> — derive from git at build time. They cannot go
        stale, because nobody types them.
      </p>

      <h2>The stack, deliberately small</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Eleventy 3 + Nunjucks</strong>, built and deployed by GitHub Actions to GitHub Pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-hosted type</strong>, latin subsets only: <a href="https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Fraunces" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fraunces</a> for display, Inter for body, IBM Plex Mono for the chrome. No font CDN, no third-party requests on the critical path.</li>
        <li><strong>Vanilla JavaScript</strong> — one small file: mobile nav, reveal-on-scroll, copy-summary, and the run-trace viewer. Every page works with JavaScript disabled.</li>
        <li><strong>Motion contract:</strong> nothing over 200ms, and <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> disables all of it.</li>
        <li><strong>Analytics:</strong> <a href="https://www.goatcounter.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GoatCounter</a> — no cookies, no fingerprinting, counts only.</li>
        <li><strong>Per-page OG images</strong> rendered from SVG templates by a build script, committed with the site.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What gets redacted, and how</h2>
      <p>
        Every case file is published against a written redaction checklist:
        customer and account identifiers removed, internal system names
        generalized, absolute volumes stated as orders of magnitude or
        multiples of baseline — while preserving the decision structure, the
        trade-offs, and the numbers already on the public record. Each case
        file carries its own redaction record and an <strong>errata section</strong>: when
        this site corrects itself, the correction is dated and left visible.
      </p>

      <h2>Lineage</h2>
      <p>
        This is the third edition of "The Hiring Brief" — same thesis, quieter
        evidence, now with a dark counterpart that reskins from the token file
        alone. Three earlier designs shipped and were retired for arguing
        louder than they proved. The repo history keeps them honest:
        <a href="{{ facts.identity.github }}/sarthak0501.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/sarthak0501/sarthak0501.github.io</a>.
      </p>
